  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1144 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nFriday through Wednesday:\n\nThe warm and unsettled weather is expected to continue across \nsouthern Wisconsin through the extended. With an upper level high \ncentered over the southern CONUS, south to southwest winds straight \nout of the Gulf will remain through the extended. Therefore, \ntemperatures in the 80s and dewpoints in the upper 60s to 70s are \nlikely to continue. With that the high heat index is likely to \npersist as well. With the sustained southwesterly flow, there will \nbe enough warm air and moisture for storms at any given time through \nthe extended. For Friday, there will be the combination of the LLJ \nduring the overnight hours with a lingering sfc boundary for day \ntime development. Similar to what we are seeing earlier in the week. \n\nOnce we get into the weekend the diurnal instability remains, but \nthere is a noticeable lack of sfc forcing. The LLJ shifts north and \nwest while the sfc frontal boundary sags to the south.\nThis will lead to more disorganized rain and storms chances. Shear \nlooks to weaken as well during this time which in turn limits the \npotential for an pesky isolated stronger storms. Realistically it \nshould close the door on it, but if there is enough diurnally driven \ninstability then we don't want to count out any single cell pop up \nthunderstorm just yet. A thunderstorm that gets tall enough or water \nlogged enough can always cause some downburst type winds. But that \nshould be very few and far between, more an exception that a rule in \nthe environment we should have. \n\nFurther ahead for early next week there is a signal for the ridge \naloft to weaken a bit. Meanwhile a low pressure system moving across \nCanada looks to potentially clip the Great Lakes Region. From the \nglobal deterministic models, the GFS looks to be the further south \nwith the low pressure system and tries to undercut the high, while \nthe Euro and CAN keep the low further north. This split in track \ncontinues when looking at the cluster analysis and ensembles. \nRegardless of track a cold front looks to move across the state with \nthis associated low. So this comes down to a timing and strength of \nthe front question as well. Overall just something to keep an eye on \nas we head into the weekend.\n\nPatterson",
